index 0000000..49f97b0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/PERFORMANCE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+The homepage loads `performance-monitor.js` with no dependencies or network reporting.
+It measures navigation link clicks, Order Now clicks, and clicks anywhere within each
+Seasonal Favorites card (including View Details and keyboard-activated links).
+
+Read the latest 100 samples in the browser Performance timeline by filtering measure
+names beginning with `daily-grind:`. Each measure's `detail` contains `kind`, `label`,
+`startTime`, `duration` (milliseconds), and `method`. To forward samples to an existing
+analytics service, subscribe to `window`'s `daily-grind:performance` event and read
+`event.detail`. No analytics service or user identifiers are configured here.
+
+Timing starts at the click event timestamp, including input delay, and ends on the
+second animation frame. This allows an intervening paint of synchronous visual
+updates; it is an estimate, not an exact presentation timestamp or an INP metric.
+It does not automatically wait for network requests, image loads, or CSS transitions.
+Samples are discarded when the page becomes hidden or unloads. Full document links
+retain their original behavior; this document cannot measure the destination page's
+paint. A click sample emitted before navigation describes only this document.
+
+History URL changes (`pushState`, `replaceState`), back/forward navigation, and hash
+changes automatically produce `view-switch` samples for the next paint opportunity.
+For asynchronous rendering or switches that do not change the URL, wrap the actual
+update so timing includes the full operation:
+
+```js
+await window.dailyGrindPerformance.measureViewSwitch('menu', async () => {
+  await renderMenu(); // Resolve after committing the DOM update.
+  // Await relevant image.decode() or Animation.finished promises here if needed.
+});
+```
+
+The wrapper preserves the update's return value and errors; errors do not produce
+successful measurements. Its promise resolves when the update completes; the timing
+event follows after the paint opportunity. A wrapped history update may produce both
+an automatic route sample and the explicit render sample; their labels distinguish
+them. No DOM content, URLs, or query parameters are included in emitted samples.
+
+Run `npm test` for deterministic Node tests of timing, navigation behavior, asynchronous
+updates, lifecycle cancellation, and retention. No server or browser is needed.
